updated: '2026-10-13'
---

Use `:RivetFile` or `rivet file` in a project or an ordinary folder. In an ordinary folder without a project marker, give the CLI `--root .` to select the destination. The editor asks for the language first, then the file arrangement. A file in the project’s primary language uses its provider’s build registration; other languages create tracked files without changing the host build. Use `--language` for the same choice in the CLI. Every operation previews its files before Neovim applies them. The build-system changes depend on the provider:

| Provider | File addition | Target addition |
| --- | --- | --- |
| C++ | Templates below, with CMake registration | Executable or static library |
| C | Source, header, or pair, with CMake registration | Executable or static library |
| Python | `.py` module in the package; import it where needed | Add an entry point in `pyproject.toml` yourself |
| Rust | `.rs` module; add its `mod` declaration yourself | Additional executable under `src/bin/` |
| Lua | Reusable module or runnable script | Library or script executable |
| Swift | `.swift` source in a recorded target directory; leaves `Package.swift` intact | Executable or library through a reviewed package-manifest update |

The sections below cover **C++**. Follow [C](/docs/c/), [Python](/docs/python/), [Rust](/docs/rust/), or [SwiftPM](/docs/swift/) for their source conventions and native ownership limits. Existing projects without `.rivet.json` can create files through the generic templates without generating a manifest or rewriting their build. Register these files with the native build yourself. See [Lua](/docs/lua/) for applications, Neovim plugins, scripting, and an optional embedded-runtime example.

## Choose a template

| Kind | Generated files |
| --- | --- |
| `source` | A `.cpp` source with the project namespace. |
| `header` | A `.hpp` header with the selected guard convention. |
| `pair` | Matching source and header files, with the source including its header. |
| `class` | A class declaration in a header and constructor definition in a source. |
| `module` | A `.cppm` interface that can contain declarations and definitions, registered in a CMake module file set. |
| `module_pair` | A `.cppm` interface with an exported function declaration and a `.cpp` implementation in the same owning target. |
| `module_impl` | A `.cpp` implementation for an existing named module. |
| `c_source` | A C17 `.c` source in the C++ project; enables C for the build. |
| `c_pair` | Matching `.c` and `.h` files with C ABI guards; enables C in the host build. |
| `c_header` | A `.h` header with `extern "C"` guards for a C ABI. |

The source/header choice is explicit: select `pair` when you want both. Module templates are available only when the project's own named modules are enabled. Standard-library imports alone do not enable named project modules.

Inside an application project named `engine`:

```sh
rivet file src/memory/page --kind pair \
  --target engine_core --preview

rivet file src/memory/page --kind pair \
  --target engine_core

rivet file Renderer --kind class --target engine_core
```

Preview inspects the complete plan without writing. Omit it to apply the CLI operation. Neovim always presents the review before applying a template.

## Understand names and paths

A bare name such as `Renderer` goes in the selected target's source directory. A name containing `/`, such as `src/memory/page`, is relative to the project root. Extensions are optional. Absolute paths, traversal, invalid identifiers, and duplicate tracked paths are rejected; existing destination files are not overwritten.

In the public-library layout, a bare header, pair, or class name puts the public header under `include/<project>/` and the implementation in the target's source directory. Additional static libraries use `include/<project>/<target>/`. An explicit relative path preserves the requested co-location.

Header guards use the project's `pragma` or `ifndef` preference. Named module units do not use header guards. In standard-library module mode, source templates place `import std;` after local includes, and module units place it after their module declaration.

New sources, headers, and module units also inherit the project's optional [file comment header](/docs/comment-headers/), with their own filename. The file-kind picker previews that header; Python, Rust, and Swift show a single **Source file** choice before asking for the path. Foreign-language files render the saved details using the selected language's comment syntax. An ordinary folder without header defaults shows an explicit not-configured message. `:RivetHeader` changes one file; `:RivetHeaderDefaults` changes what future templates use in a Rivet-owned project.

## Add a module pair or a separate implementation

Choose **Module interface + implementation** to create both together:

```sh
rivet file src/memory/pool --kind module_pair \
  --module engine.memory.pool --target engine_core
```

Alternatively, create the interface first and add its implementation later. Use these commands instead of the pair above; the same module name and destination cannot be created twice:

```sh
rivet file src/memory/pool --kind module \
  --module engine.memory.pool --target engine_core

rivet file src/memory/pool_impl --kind module_impl \
  --module engine.memory.pool --target engine_core
```

The `module` command creates an interface skeleton. Put function bodies there for a single-file module, or declarations there and definitions in the `module_impl` file. Do not define the same function in both. The implementation must refer to an existing module and belong to its owning target. In Neovim, selecting the module automatically selects that target. The declaration and definition syntax is explained in [Modules and headers](/docs/modules/).

## Add a build target

An application scaffold named `engine` creates `engine_core`, `engine`, and `engine_test`. A library scaffold creates `engine` and `engine_test`.

```sh
rivet target renderer --kind library --link-to engine
rivet target viewer --kind app
```

The first command creates a static library and links the existing `engine` target to it. The second creates an independent executable. Adding a target does not automatically move existing files or infer application dependencies. Use the final CMake review to inspect the relationships, and keep custom build extensions in `CMakeLists.txt`.

## Files in another language

```sh
rivet file tools/analyze --language python --kind source --preview
rivet file scripts/prepare --language lua --kind script --preview
rivet file lua/engine/helper --language lua --kind module --preview
rivet file notes.txt --language text --kind source --preview
```

Mixed C files in a C++ project can join an existing CMake target with `--language c --kind pair --target engine_core`. Other foreign files are recorded for later move/remove operations, without adding a new build target. They are yours to connect to the host application. An explicit path is relative to the project root; Lua bare module names use `lua/<project>/` and scripts use `scripts/`.
